{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "1 - Notifyd Codebase Overview",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Welcome",
      "description": "# Welcome to Notifyd\n\nOn this tour we will take you on a quick overview of the codebase, from deployment configuration down to tests.\n\nText like this is executable in a terminal or just by clicking it:\n\n>> script/bootstrap\n\nIn fact, if you haven't already, now is a good time to bootstrap so why not click the command above before continuing."
    },
    {
      "file": "docs/adr/0001-record-architecture-decisions.md",
      "description": "## docs/adr - Architecture Decision Records\n\nWe try to document any architectural, or otherwise significant, decisions in ADRs. Please don't be afraid to create more, they don't have to be long.\n\nIf you ran `script/bootstrap` already you should have adr-tools installed, so can start a new adr with `adr new Decide to do something`.",
      "line": 2
    },
    {
      "directory": "config",
      "description": "## config/ - Deployment Configuration\n\n`config/` and it's subdirectories are how we configure kubernetes deployments on our internal infrastructure \"moda\".\n\nSee https://moda.githubapp.com/docs for moda docs,\nand https://moda.githubapp.com/apps/notifyd for the service dashboard.",
      "title": "config/ - deployment configuration"
    },
    {
      "directory": "cmd",
      "description": "## cmd/ - Go binary entrypoints\n\n`cmd/` is where all our binary entrypoints live. These are what will be compiled into executables when running `script/build`.",
      "title": "cmd/ - go binaries entry points"
    },
    {
      "file": "cmd/api/main.go",
      "description": "## Twirp API\n\n`cmd/api/` is where our twirp api is configured and started.\n\nThe majority of the code for implementing handlers lives in `internal/api`, but extra handler endpoints, or configuration for the api server as a whole should live in here/.",
      "pattern": "func main()"
    },
    {
      "file": "cmd/notify-consumer/notify-consumer.go",
      "description": "## Notify consumer\n\nEach hydro consumer also has it's own binary, and thus its own `cmd/`.\n\nThis consumer is responsible for consuming the `Notify` hydro messages. The code in `cmd` is mostly setup and configuration, with the business logic happening in packages in `internal/`.",
      "pattern": "func main()"
    },
    {
      "file": "cmd/deliver-mobile-push-consumer/deliver-mobile-push-consumer.go",
      "description": "## Deliver Mobile Push Consumer\n\nAnd the DeliverMobilePushConsumer has its own `cmd` too. You will note that much of the code here is very similar boilerplate to the `Notify` consumer with the exception of this line where we call out to the internal mobile delivery consumer package to do the work.",
      "pattern": "mobileDeliveryConsumer\\.HandleMessage"
    },
    {
      "directory": "internal",
      "description": "## Code goes here! - `internal/`\n\n`internal/` is where the source for all our internal go packages lives. If you are writing new business logic in go, it probably goes here.",
      "title": "`internal/` - code goes here! internal packages"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/notify/consumer.go",
      "description": "## internal/ package example\n\nHere's an example of an internal package - here's the package that implements the business logic from the notify consumer `cmd` we learned about earlier.",
      "line": 38,
      "pattern": "func ([^)]*) HandleMessage"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/notify/consumer_test.go",
      "description": "## Internal package tests\n\nTest for packages in go live alongside the source code - they even share a `package` declaration - so they are easy to find.\n\nTest functions should all start like `func Test...` and take `(t *testing.T)` as their argument.\n\n### Running Tests\n\nYou can run tests with:\n\n>> script/test\n\n(Click above to try it!)",
      "pattern": "func TestHandleMessage"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/notify/consumer_test.go",
      "description": "## Table driven tests\n\nIf you want to test a number of different cases, it's best to build a slice of test conditions and assertions and iterate over them like this to perform the test, rather than creating a `Test` function for each one.\n\nBy default go will _not_ exit the test function on it's first failed assertion so you will still learn which of your tables passed or failed.\n\nLearn more about [table driven tests](https://dave.cheney.net/2019/05/07/prefer-table-driven-tests)\n\n",
      "pattern": "tests := \\[\\]struct"
    },
    {
      "directory": "proto",
      "description": "## proto/ - protobuf schemas\n\nThe `proto/` directory contains our protobuf schemas. If you are adding new layouts or twirp endpoints you'll probably start here."
    },
    {
      "directory": "ruby",
      "description": "## ruby/ - our rubygem\n\nWe publish a rubygem to make it easy for the monolith or other ruby projects to consume our twirp api and layouts.\n\nThe code for that gem lives in this directory."
    },
    {
      "directory": "vendor",
      "description": "## vendor/ - vendored go dependencies\n\nWe vendor the source of any packages we depend on into the vendor directory. This creates some noise in PRs, but such is life.\n\nYou should not need to modify files in this directory. If you have added/removed a dependency you can run this command to revendor:\n\n>> script/vendor"
    },
    {
      "title": "Next steps",
      "description": "## Next steps\n\n- Learn about [the Twirp API](command:codetour.startTourByTitle?[\"Twirp API\"])\n- or, [Restart this tour](command:codetour.startTourByTitle?[\"Notifyd Codebase Overview\"])"
    }
  ],
  "isPrimary": true
}
